  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThe main forecast concern will continue to be the prolonged well \nabove normal temperatures. This will come courtesy of a upper level \nridge whose axis will be centered over the area today into tomorrow. \nThis coupled with modest WAA will allow highs to rise into the mid \n90s to the low 100s today and tomorrow. Fortunately, dewpoints in \nthe low to mid-60s will hold heat index values in the 100 to 105 \nrange. By Wednesday, the ridge axis will slide eastward into the \nOhio and Tennessee River Valleys. However, the local area will \nremain under the influence of this feature keeping highs in the mid \n90s to near 100. Although temperatures may be a few degrees cooler \nWednesday due to minor height falls, moisture will slowly rise \nacross the area with dewpoints creeping into the mid to upper 60s. \nThis will yield continued heat index values between 100-105 with \nsome areas south of the Missouri rising above 105. These very hot \ndaytime conditions coupled with nighttime lows providing little \nrelief remaining in the mid 70s to lower 80s in the urban core of \nKansas City has prompted the issuance of a heat advisory today \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nThe best chance for precipitation this week across the forecast area \nwill come Wednesday night into Thursday morning as the upper level \nridge slides far enough eastward where the local area is on the \nwestern periphery. A couple weak shortwaves rounding the ridge may \nbring a few showers (15-25%) to far northern Missouri otherwise, \nexpect conditions to continue to remain dry and hot. Highs Thursday \nwill remain in the mid to upper 90 and heat index values will remain \nin the 100-105 range which may prompt an upgrade to a excessive heat \nwarning (due to the duration of the heat to 4 days or more). Friday, \nthe upper level ridge begins to retrograde back to the west with the \nridge axis moving back over the local area. Highs again will be in \nthe mid to upper 90s with heat indices in the 100-105 range. Perhaps \nthe best chance for precipitation this forecast period will come \nnext weekend when shortwaves rounding the ridge acts to try and \nflatten it bringing slight (15-25%) storm chances. This however, \nwill remain to be seen, as while the GFS favors the aforementioned \nsolution, the EC depicts a much stronger and amplified ridge than \nthe GFS which would keep any precipitation well north of the\narea.",
